WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.600
- Thanks. I'm gonna draw together some of

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.270
what my colleagues today have said

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.750
and then some of what we heard yesterday,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:20.630
especially in the session on climate.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.750
I'm going to talk briefly, I think,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.650
about kind of the underlying, you know,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.790
kind of essence and characteristics

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.810
of what we call the global nuclear order.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:32.970
And then I'm going to try to explore

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.190
what I think are some of
the, again, deeper causes

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.270
of the disorder that my
colleagues have talked about.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.670
And then, briefly suggest
some things that can be done,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.523
that kind of build on or add
to what they had talked about.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.800
Michael, you know, began by talking about,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.567
in essence, you know, at
least one of the pillars

00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:01.340
of what became a global
nuclear order after 1945.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.036
And he talked about how remarkable it is

00:01:04.036 --> 00:01:06.966
that nuclear weapons hadn't been detonated

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:11.930
in anger against targets since 1945.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.020
And I think that's the essence

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.077
of the accomplishment of the nuclear war,

00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:18.280
but there were additional facets to it.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.890
There was a tremendous worry

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.780
that nuclear weapons would
proliferate to other countries.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.420
So, famously, President
Kennedy's administration,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.820
President Kennedy was briefed

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.610
that 25 countries would
acquire nuclear weapons

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:36.610
very quickly, very soon if
things continued as they would.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.540
And, so for a variety of reasons,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.610
particularly the US and the Soviet Union

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:47.610
cooperated to create the nuclear
non-proliferation treaty,

00:01:47.961 --> 00:01:52.575
and that was negotiated, finally, in 1968.

00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:54.580
And, basically, since then,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.580
since that treaty was done and
entered into force in 1970,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.780
there's only been one country
that signed the treaty,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.150
then cheated on it and
acquired nuclear weapons,

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.570
and that's North Korea.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.450
It's quite a remarkable achievement.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.500
If you think about international treaties,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.380
you think about anything,

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.101
you think about domestic
legislation and crime,

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:18.180
and to have, you know, that rate,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.630
that small rate of violation, you know,

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.870
that somebody gets away
with, is quite remarkable.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.450
And, so, that was another element

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.790
of this global nuclear order.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.826
And then, the third element of it

00:02:31.826 --> 00:02:36.050
was peaceful cooperation
and atomic energy.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.100
So if you think about it,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.660
and I'm going to come to this later,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:43.069
because today nuclear
energy isn't very attractive

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:48.069
except in a few non-market
societies or economies

00:02:48.718 --> 00:02:51.391
but there are lots of,

00:02:51.391 --> 00:02:55.580
I should know how many nuclear
power plants in the world,

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.370
but it's around 450 thereabouts

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:01.640
and in many countries,

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.524
and that operation has gone relatively,

00:03:05.524 --> 00:03:06.980
well it's gone very well

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.100
in terms of people cheating
and building nuclear weapons

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.540
out of those civilian nuclear programs.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.500
And that was another part

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.400
of this nuclear order that was created.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.880
And, I guess what I want to say about it,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.378
it's been a remarkable success

00:03:20.378 --> 00:03:24.530
and it's what was key to it,

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.950
is it's built on reliable reciprocity.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.080
That's an expression that I
borrowed from Lauren Berlant,

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.736
who is a, she's like a literary theorist

00:03:34.736 --> 00:03:36.170
at the University of Chicago,

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.990
that I had never heard
of until two weeks ago

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.840
and then I checked out
a book that she wrote

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.270
and a lot of it's hard to read

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.660
but then there's some really
brilliant stuff in it too

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:45.880
that I can understand.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:46.713
And one of these,

00:03:46.713 --> 00:03:50.260
and I used to talk about
sustainable reciprocity,

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.465
but she has reliable reciprocity,
which is alliterative,

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:54.900
and it's just great.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.263
So, I stole it and I'll quit
footnoting it hereafter,

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.240
but that's what it was built upon,

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.610
that if you restrain yourself,

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.200
if the weapon States
restrained themselves,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.439
if they agree to work
towards nuclear disarmament

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:10.200
and others restrain themselves

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.770
from acquiring nuclear weapons

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:12.980
and you play by the rules,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.310
everybody does better through
that kind of reciprocity.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:18.821
And it's some of what we
talked about yesterday

00:04:18.821 --> 00:04:21.056
and other elements of multilateralism,

00:04:21.056 --> 00:04:24.720
it's central to multilateralism.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.763
It was key to the nuclear order working,

00:04:28.253 --> 00:04:30.530
as I said, the non-proliferation treaty

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:31.810
was the essence of it

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:32.643
and so here I'll borrow

00:04:32.643 --> 00:04:34.587
from something Kevin Rudd said last night

00:04:34.587 --> 00:04:36.718
about the UN, which was based-

00:04:36.718 --> 00:04:39.550
he said, look if the UN didn't exist

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.100
we'd have to invent something like it.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.803
Well, if the non-proliferation
treaty didn't exist

00:04:43.803 --> 00:04:46.540
we'd have to invent something like it,

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.820
except today it will be
much, much harder to invent

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.490
and get international
subscription for than it was then.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:00.227
So in many ways, it's
all the more important to

00:05:00.227 --> 00:05:05.227
to defend and to invest in restoring.

00:05:05.666 --> 00:05:08.650
And the last point to,
again, set the stage

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:11.119
about order and how important
the order is in here,

00:05:11.119 --> 00:05:14.590
in here I'm picking up on something

00:05:15.563 --> 00:05:18.453
Professor Yan said, which is,

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.440
look at how we're worried

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.380
about artificial intelligence today,

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.600
cyber more broadly, but
artificial intelligence,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.440
especially, has people very concerned.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.290
Whether it's automated weapons
systems, you know, aircraft,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.310
everything people are worried about,

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:36.160
especially the international dimension.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.100
And there is no order there.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.950
There's no domestic regulation,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:43.240
there's no understanding
of liability domestically.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.870
Internationally, they haven't
even begun discussions of it.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:50.660
And so, when you think,
you juxtapose that kind

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.160
of nascent stage of technology burgeoning,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.970
lots of concern about it,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.360
there's a sense that
it needs to be ordered.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.170
No one has the slightest idea
how it's going to be done.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:05.240
Well, that was kind of
where nuclear began in 1945

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.561
and so it's quite remarkable how quickly

00:06:07.561 --> 00:06:12.320
and successfully an order
was built in that domain.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.570
And we should hope that we can figure out

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.970
how to do as well with AI and
some of these technologies.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.360
But if that's going to be the case,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.170
I think we're going to have to go back

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:22.480
to the fundamental principles

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.868
of reliable reciprocity, quid pro quos,

00:06:25.868 --> 00:06:30.267
and creating incentives
for people to comply.

00:06:30.267 --> 00:06:32.710
So, that leads me to the second section,

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:36.200
which is, my sense that,

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.540
that what we're experiencing
in a lot of the problems

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.770
that people here talked about,

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.791
whether it's the INF treaty
being first violated,

00:06:43.791 --> 00:06:47.850
and then, you know, withdrawn from,

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.402
the absence of arms
control, the DPRK challenge,

00:06:51.402 --> 00:06:56.402
the Iran undoing, there's
a long list, the arms race.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.030
I think what's happening,

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:03.030
is you have disinvestment
from this nuclear order,

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:08.370
and disinvestment from at
least two blocks of country.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.760
And I'll suggest, there are
four factors driving it.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.563
So the US, and I would say, Russia,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.219
are disinvesting from that nuclear order

00:07:20.219 --> 00:07:22.570
for somewhat similar reasons.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:27.570
The US disinvestment is really intensified

00:07:27.584 --> 00:07:31.410
and made explicit by John
Bolton and President Trump.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.756
But really, John Bolton makes it explicit.

00:07:33.756 --> 00:07:35.256
And the idea is, look,

00:07:35.256 --> 00:07:40.220
there aren't many threats
of proliferation anymore.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.160
All right, like name the
country that we're now worried

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.729
about will acquire nuclear weapons.

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:47.906
The one that people
nominate is Saudi Arabia.

00:07:47.906 --> 00:07:49.900
Well, first of all, from
the Bush administration

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.790
that isn't the biggest
problem in the world.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.070
So then, the next thing is, well okay,

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:55.360
so, tell me a bad guy

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.900
who's going to get nuclear weapons now.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.810
And the answer of, I'm sorry,
of the Trump administration

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:04.961
and John Bolton is we'll handle
that ourselves, militarily.

00:08:04.961 --> 00:08:06.934
So, we don't need all these rules

00:08:06.934 --> 00:08:09.396
and this rigmarole and all these bargains

00:08:09.396 --> 00:08:13.010
and concessions and
trade-offs, you know, screw it.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:14.660
If somebody does it, we'll remove them

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.670
and that was basically the
Bush administration strategy

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.540
in 2002, with the axis of evil.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:22.369
That's the strategy
that led to the invasion

00:08:22.369 --> 00:08:25.990
of Iraq and Iran was supposed to be next,

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:27.640
you know, or North Korea.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.560
And so, the approach is you don't,

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.240
it's like the NRA says about arms control,

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.210
the problem isn't arms,
it's not nuclear arms.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.560
The problem is bad guys with nuclear arms

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:41.230
and so the strategy is
not to control the arms,

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.060
it's to get rid of the bad guys.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:45.990
And so, it's a coherent theory

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:49.145
that leads to disinvestment
from that kind of order,

00:08:49.145 --> 00:08:51.191
and I think Russia is disinvesting

00:08:51.191 --> 00:08:53.490
for somewhat related reasons.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:56.131
Russia is more concerned about
selling nuclear power plants,

00:08:56.131 --> 00:08:58.180
about making deals in countries

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:01.310
that may be seeking to
acquire nuclear energy.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.658
And if there's a worry
that those countries,

00:09:03.658 --> 00:09:06.770
like Saudi Arabia, may seek
to acquire nuclear weapons,

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:08.020
we'll deal with that later,

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.830
or that will be the US's problem

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:13.940
or somebody else's problem,
but it's not the first concern.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.410
And beyond sales, the bigger concern

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.042
is contesting and countering the US

00:09:19.042 --> 00:09:22.364
and trying to play off
challenges with other countries

00:09:22.364 --> 00:09:26.345
as a way to gain leverage over the US.

00:09:26.345 --> 00:09:29.780
I think the second factor
causing disinvestment

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.979
is that nuclear energy
is no longer attractive.

00:09:33.979 --> 00:09:35.618
It's not attractive enough

00:09:35.618 --> 00:09:37.890
for developing countries to make them go,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.856
gee, I want nuclear energy so badly,

00:09:40.856 --> 00:09:43.930
that I'll do whatever these
people are telling me to do,

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.505
in terms of export controls,

00:09:45.505 --> 00:09:49.950
not acquiring nuclear
weapons, behaving well,

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.743
so that I can get nuclear energy.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.890
Look at it now and I'd
say after Fukushima,

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.370
especially, but also just prices,

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:00.210
it's just not a great investment.

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:01.479
China's the only country, really,

00:10:01.479 --> 00:10:02.803
that's got a kind of,

00:10:02.803 --> 00:10:06.860
a still a burgeoning
nuclear energy program.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.060
And so, I think that changes the dynamic

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:12.350
within the nuclear order.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:16.330
The third, kind of,
disinvesting, you know,

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:20.350
dynamic is that the
disadvantaged participants

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.750
in this order, are ones
that feel they're victims,

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.530
are primarily the
non-nuclear weapons States.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:27.888
The countries in South America, Africa,

00:10:27.888 --> 00:10:31.361
and parts of Asia that
feel they get no benefit

00:10:31.361 --> 00:10:33.153
from nuclear deterrence.

00:10:33.153 --> 00:10:33.986
All right.

00:10:33.986 --> 00:10:36.240
So, you know, unlike our NATO allies,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.837
US-NATO allies, unlike Russia,
these countries go look,

00:10:38.837 --> 00:10:40.640
we don't face threats

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.930
for which nuclear deterrence is relevant.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:45.067
These countries aren't gonna defend us.

00:10:45.067 --> 00:10:49.110
And yet, if they have a big
nuclear war, we get screwed.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.610
Like, our food supply is
going to be endangered.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:53.910
We are going to be dealing

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:56.750
with the global chaos
that will come with that.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:00.810
And so, they say, we want
to morally protest this,

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:05.340
we want to disinvest and
challenge this system.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.150
And so, they basically led
negotiation two years ago

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:10.955
of a treaty to prohibit nuclear weapons.

00:11:10.955 --> 00:11:12.090
All right.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.053
Frustrated with the
non-proliferation treaty,

00:11:14.053 --> 00:11:15.603
feeling that the nuclear weapons states

00:11:15.603 --> 00:11:17.519
are never going to get
rid of nuclear weapons.

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:19.270
They said to hell with it, we'll just,

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.940
we'll negotiate a separate treaty,

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:24.030
that'll just ban them,
prohibit them, period.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:26.200
It doesn't matter if it's not binding

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:30.752
on any of the weapons states,
we'll make our stand on there.

00:11:30.752 --> 00:11:35.094
And we're less interested
now in non-proliferation.

00:11:35.094 --> 00:11:39.990
I think the fourth driver
of this disinvestment

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.190
is something that we've
been talking about,

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.320
which is new technology.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:45.992
So, whether it's artificial intelligence,

00:11:45.992 --> 00:11:49.110
hypersonic conventional weapons,

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:50.640
that Nikolai talked about,

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.830
other kinds of systems,
people are kind of going,

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:55.034
those are the new threats

00:11:55.034 --> 00:11:57.380
that we really gotta be worried about.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:58.980
Nuclear is kind of the old,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.171
it's not clear how nuclear arms control

00:12:01.171 --> 00:12:03.190
and the existing systems going to deal

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.112
with these new technologies.

00:12:05.112 --> 00:12:07.289
So, let's focus on the new technologies

00:12:07.289 --> 00:12:11.770
and spend less time and
energy on the old ones.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:13.451
So, from different directions,

00:12:13.451 --> 00:12:15.580
you've got some people arguing from, like,

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:18.820
a security narrative saying
they're disinvesting,

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.630
others from a moral or
humanitarian narrative

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:24.653
saying they're disinvesting
from this order.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:27.696
And all of that is compounded

00:12:27.696 --> 00:12:29.999
by the lack of leadership from the US

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:33.020
because new orders, basically,

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.730
whether they're trained, as
we talked about yesterday,

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:39.470
or climate and nuclear, you need at least,

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:42.068
like one of the biggest
powers to try to drive it,

00:12:42.068 --> 00:12:44.320
preferably you'd have two.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:46.190
So the nuclear war was built by the US

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:48.320
and the Soviet Union, we talked yesterday,

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.076
Todd talked about, you can't do climate

00:12:50.076 --> 00:12:52.670
without the US and China.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:55.166
I would argue, I mean, both
of those things are true

00:12:55.166 --> 00:12:58.523
and on the nuclear, we're
going to have to have the US,

00:12:58.523 --> 00:13:00.783
Russia, and China going forward.

00:13:00.783 --> 00:13:02.442
If you don't have that cooperation

00:13:02.442 --> 00:13:06.020
you can't, basically, you
know, restore the order.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.803
So that leads to what can we do about it?

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:12.165
In my mind, you know, the first thing is

00:13:12.165 --> 00:13:15.177
something that none of
my colleagues think about

00:13:15.177 --> 00:13:17.390
and that Washington
doesn't think about at all.

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:21.860
Except maybe President Trump
and this is a serious comment,

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:23.080
and kind of praise of him.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:24.640
It's not praise, but it's an accident,

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:29.640
but how do you get Russia
off the sanctions track?

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:34.928
So Russia had a bunch of
sanctions imposed deservedly,

00:13:34.928 --> 00:13:37.700
in my view, for what it did in Ukraine.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:41.291
But the problem with sanctions
is once you've applied them

00:13:41.291 --> 00:13:44.720
you've kind of defeated the purpose,

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:46.670
the behavior that you
are trying to change,

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:47.503
you know, doesn't work.

00:13:47.503 --> 00:13:50.570
So in Russia's case,
how do we move forward?

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:52.600
How does the world move forward?

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:54.710
Russia is never giving back Crimea.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:55.780
I mean, never is a long time.

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:59.333
It's not clear when and why
Russia would give back Crimea.

00:13:59.333 --> 00:14:02.140
It's still meddling in Ukraine.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:03.890
The sanctions are there.

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:05.680
Putin is really pissed off about it.

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.340
He wants the sanctions removed.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:09.509
There's no dialogue or
even thinking in the west,

00:14:09.509 --> 00:14:11.860
how to remove the sanctions.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.010
And so if you want to get
Russia to come back in

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:15.420
and play on these other issues,

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:17.360
how do you motivate them to do that?

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:18.600
Seems to me a vital issue

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:20.669
that no one's really thinking about.

00:14:20.669 --> 00:14:22.510
And then with China,

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:27.510
how do you deal with the
territorial aggrandizement

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:32.220
that has people so concerned
in the South China seas?

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:36.969
There's a building of islands
out of rock outcroppings

00:14:36.969 --> 00:14:41.969
or adding of runways
on to things that alarm

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:45.793
ASEAN States, Japan, the US, and others.

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.300
We have to deal with the-

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:49.550
And then China's concerned

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:51.534
about what US intentions are in Taiwan.

00:14:51.534 --> 00:14:54.191
If you don't address
those underlying issues.

00:14:54.191 --> 00:14:56.440
It seems to me it's very hard to then get

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.521
into a serious discussion
about strategic stability

00:15:00.521 --> 00:15:04.280
and tempering or controlling the military,

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:06.270
including the nuclear competition.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:07.592
But again, we don't have a dialogue

00:15:07.592 --> 00:15:09.560
with China on strategic stability.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:13.230
We don't, we're not
engaged in that process.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.380
And that was a problem before
the Trump administration

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:18.006
and it's been exacerbated by Trump.

00:15:18.006 --> 00:15:20.210
So I've got a list of other things

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:21.043
that are very similar

00:15:21.043 --> 00:15:23.700
to what came up in the climate discussion

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.771
about accountability and equity.

00:15:25.771 --> 00:15:28.920
So there are larger
things than arms control

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:31.120
but it seems to me if
we don't address those,

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:34.950
the multilateral order
around nuclear weapons

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.560
is going to continue to
experience disinvestment.

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:38.885
And so I'll stop there.

00:15:38.885 --> 00:15:41.468
- [Announcer] Okay. Thank you.

